schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "The History of Bitcoin"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    A chronological explainer of Bitcoin's origins, foundations-track intro
    article: the pre-Bitcoin digital-cash experiments (Chaum's ecash, Back's
    hashcash, Dai's b-money, Szabo's bit gold, Finney's RPOW), Satoshi
    Nakamoto's October 2008 whitepaper solving the double-spend problem
    without a central authority, the January 2009 genesis block, the 2010
    Bitcoin Pizza Day transaction and the August 2010 value-overflow bug and
    its fix, Bitcoin's 2011-2012 mainstream milestones, and Satoshi's
    disappearance. Frames Bitcoin as the culmination of 30+ years of attempts
    to build money that doesn't depend on central authorities.
  audience: newcomers to crypto/web3 in a foundations learning track
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      each pioneer (Chaum, Back, Dai, Szabo, Finney) solved one piece of the
      digital-cash puzzle but needed a central authority or never launched;
      the article frames Bitcoin's contribution as synthesis, not any single
      novel idea.
  related_concepts:
    - double-spend-problem-and-proof-of-work-lineage
  related_resources: []
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/bitcoin-history.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-648651c05dac
  source_lineage: bitcoin-history.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - Named individuals (Satoshi Nakamoto, Hal Finney, Wei Dai, Nick Szabo,
      Adam Back, David Chaum, Laszlo Hanyecz, Gavin Andresen) are treated as
      already-public, extensively documented historical/crypto-history
      figures rather than community members whose profiles need protecting —
      no public-person-node created and no high_risk flag set, since this is
      canonical, widely-published history rather than a community identity
      disclosure.
    - No exact locations, TEK/Indigenous knowledge, MRV/carbon claims, or
      funding/legal/governance recommendations present.
